# An allowlist, not a denylist — deliberately.
#
# Next's file tracer, unable to resolve `resolve(process.cwd(), dir, …)`
# statically, copies broadly: a build here swept the entire project root into
# `.next/standalone`, including the statement of work and the private ownership
# records. Nothing in the image needed either.
#
# A denylist would have had to name them, and would have to name the next such
# file too — the one added months from now by somebody who never read this. So
# everything is excluded and the build's actual inputs are named back in. A new
# file in the repository root does not reach an image until someone says so.
#
# Check what a build would send, without building:
#   git ls-files --others --cached --exclude-standard | git check-ignore -v --no-index --stdin
*

# --- what the build genuinely needs -----------------------------------------
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!next.config.ts
!tsconfig.json
!src

# The issuer's public statements: a town, a bedroom count, a commitment, a
# signature. They are served by the app and the verify screen needs them.
!inventory/attestations

# Rights 5-7 were issued by `npm run evidence`, which files their attestations
# apart so evidence runs stay disposable. They are on the same contract and in
# the same registry, so an image without them shows three weeks as never
# attested — and an unattested week is kept out of the shopping filters, so it
# is not a cosmetic difference. Attestations only: `inventory/evidence/canonical`
# alongside it holds the records themselves and must never ship.
!inventory/evidence
!inventory/evidence/attestations

# --- and, explicitly, what must never be in an image ------------------------
# Already covered by `*`. Repeated because these are the ones that would matter,
# and a reader should be able to see the intent without re-deriving it.
#
#   2.pdf, quietstay_phase1_build_prompt.md   the statement of work
#   .env, .env.local, .env*.local             every secret
#   inventory/records, inventory/canonical    names, resorts, units, deeds, salts
#   inventory/requests                        who asked whom for what
#   .git                                      the history of all of the above
